==English==

===Etymology===
Figurative, from the image of competing horses [[abreast]] in a [[horse race]] who are so [[even]] (headed for a [[tie#English:_draw between competitors|tie]]) that their [[neck]]s are [[side-by-side]].

===Phrase===
{{head|en|phrase}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} Very [[close]] in [[progress]], as in a [[race]] or a [[contest]].
#: {{syn|en|nip and tuck}}
#: {{ux|en|The polls suggest that the candidates were '''neck and neck''' in the election.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Love à-la-mode, etc.|author=Charles Macklin|page=20|year=1779|passage=It lay between me and Dick Riot madam; we were '''neck and neck''' for three miles, as hard as we could lay leg to ground, and running every inch, but at the first, I felt for him, found I had the foot {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|magazine=Sporting Magazine|passage=Mr. Prendergast's Rainbow filly, watched closely by Paul Jones, took the lead, and they ran almost '''neck and neck''' until near the cords, when Paul Jones made a rush, and came home by a length.|page=45|title=Sporting Intelligence|volume=17|year=1825|month=November|issue=98}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=May and June|title=Notes and News: Express Travel on Slow Lines|journal=Railway Magazine|page=184|text=[...] Mr. M. N. Rollason points out that on four-track lines on which the fast lines, in the centre, are flanked by the slow lines, and running at speed is permissible on all four, the traveller can enjoy some quite exciting experiences when trains are doing a "'''neck-and-neck'''" on adjacent lines.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=July|title=|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=410, photo caption|text=Passing Iver, W.R. [[4-6-0]] No. 1012 ''County of Denbigh'' on the up "Capitals United Express" runs '''neck-and-neck''' with a parcels train headed by 4-6-0 No. 6821 ''Leaton Grange''.}}

